A||||9||AkursedX||Can Oil Pump be Changed With Engine Still in Car?||||5||
Z||000000||AkursedX||11-07-2001||06:52 PM||||Well I asked my question in the title.  So can it be?  The car in question is an 87GT.  I remember reading something about it before but I can't find the topic.<P>And if it can be done. Can someone give the procedure for it?  I do have an engine hoist so I can pick up the motor. <P>Any feedback would be greatly appreciated.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||avengador1||11-07-2001||07:28 PM||||You have to raise the car high enough to work under it. Next empty the oil pan and remove it. The oil pump is accesible now, just unbolt it and remove. when putting the new oil pump in you need to press the pick up tube into the pump body. It helps if you warm the pump body in the oven to make it expand and put the pick up tube in the freezer to make it shrink. This will give you a little more clearance to assemble these parts. You still will have to hammer them together as it is a really tight fit. Don't forget the drive shaft for the distributor before installing the pump. It has a plastic snap fitting to hold it in the pump. It might be easier to remove the distributor but I feel you should be able to fish this shft back up into the distributor. I hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||3
Z||000002||DJRice||11-07-2001||08:13 PM||||It seems to me that there might be some issues with slipping the oil pan down far enough to clear the crankshaft because of the crossmember on the engine cradle, but its been a while since I actually had to climb under the car to do any work.<P>&lt;knock on wood&gt;<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/djr_signature.jpg"> <BR>"And this one time, on the Fiero Forum..."||||reg||1
Z||000003||avengador1||11-08-2001||09:31 PM||||I just went to recheck my 87 GT and from what I could see, peeking under the car, there is nothing in the way from dropping the oil pan down. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000004||Neal||11-09-2001||01:04 AM||||there is everything in the way, motor mount, exaust, you can do it but it is NOT fun.||||reg||1
Z||000005||jfuredy||11-09-2001||03:42 PM||||Actually, avengador1, you need to cool both parts.  If you heat a large block of metal that has a hole in the middle, the hole will actually get SMALLER.  This is because the metal around the hole gets bigger, and encroaches in on the hole.<P>I was very surprised by this fact when I first learned it in my materials class, but now that I think about it, it makes perfect sense.<P>Just thought I'd share my experience.<BR>Joe Furedy<BR>'88 Formula<BR>Phoenix, AZ||||reg||1
Z||000006||avengador1||11-09-2001||06:08 PM||||That's the way I put mine together. I had read about it in one of my hot rod magazines and that is what they had suggested. I know it would not go together when both pieces were at room temperature without a press of some sort. I work with injection molds that have cores that have to slide in holes with less than .oo1" clearance. As the metal in the cores heats up it expands(all our molds are heated by temperature controllers, and the hot plastic that is injected into them, some plastics we use run as high as 630 degrees F.). If what you say is true, then the holes these cores slide thru would contract, as the steel heats up, causing interference or binding. I have never seen this happen. I also remember from my material science class if you wanted to assemble a pin in a hole so it would not come out, you would make the pin O.D. slightly oversize than the hole and you could freeze it to make it go in the hole without needing a press. once the temperature of both pieces of metal equalized you would not be able to pull the pin out, because of the interference fit.||||reg||1
Z||000007||avengador1||11-09-2001||06:11 PM||||I guess the best would be to just try and cool the tube before trying to press it into the pump body||||reg||1
Z||000008||MrPAW||11-09-2001||06:14 PM||||It doesn't matter how large the block of metal is - if you heat the entire piece of metal evenly (such as placing it in an oven) the hole will get larger.<P>If you heat just the area around the hole (like with a torch) the hole will get smaller as jfuerdy stated.<P>My father used this practice in tool and die shops for what he called a "permanent press" fit.  They used induction heaters and liquid nitrogen to get the maximum amouts of contraction and expansion.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||avengador1||11-09-2001||08:13 PM||||Thanks for clearing up the point. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
